#{[17592186527194 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527194 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527194 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527194 #edb/ref 1071 "How is the angle ${120}^{∘}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527194 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a843ee14-f56b-58b5-bfd0-4708e1493281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527194 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/72/72030fb9fe1e92b8be67d4a4cf2c899c83ba1242892231f18925c2354a143bdc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527203 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527203 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527203 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ca/caacb6ef264b8f7862a893b4127704e46e66d33a212b53017e8bde4e32fa3004.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{2}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527203 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527203 #edb/ref 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/43/432dbcd0967ba40a5f477d30c8e9b0985c8151fb2f13e54ad346cff4ac20c1c5.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527204 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527204 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527204 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1bc441dcf36de00672be1ac0ff5004e25ffb04d64788a86df3c849da0600794a.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{5}{4}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527204 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527204 #edb/ref 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/e3/e3abf66be20a5ab3ed5d0b8812b127d6336cb88f8369937d415c8918d020e45e.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{5}{4})}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={45}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={225}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527215 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527215 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527215 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/08/08ee9a92a96f2ab0e4644dc02dfd4be605e72e9b778f54bf60d8e4014d58a7bd.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},9)$, calculate the angle $\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527215 #edb/ref 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is supplementary to $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/e0/e0643cdb2d864b3af6466e24ab815dc8085e811dfdb86cdce253981a521faf82.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{9}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\theta +{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi ,$ then $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
